glibc 2.43 (commit cd748a6) makes strchr const-preserving under C23: when
the input is const-qualified, the result is const char *. GCC 15 builds
the minimal examples with -Werror=discarded-qualifiers.

In the default build the only failure is protocol_lws_deaddrop.c, which
casts the mutable fname[] buffer to const char * and then assigns
strchr() to char *wp so it can write the terminator. Drop that cast.
The earlier owner scan already stores its strchr() result in const char *.

Upstream 54e67208976494a4a2235f2e0fc959d46bcb7452 fixes the same glibc
change in other files and is already present in v4.5.8. It does not touch
this file, and no later main commit fixes it without an unrelated rewrite.

--- a/plugins/deaddrop/protocol_lws_deaddrop.c
+++ b/plugins/deaddrop/protocol_lws_deaddrop.c
@@ -697,7 +697,7 @@
 	}
 
 	lws_strncpy(fname, ((const char *)in) + 8, sizeof(fname));
-	wp = strchr((const char *)fname, '\"');
+	wp = strchr(fname, '\"');
 	if (wp)
 		*wp = '\0';
 
